{"kind":"assert","text":"Settling the one part of verdict 38af9070 that turns on my own wording, so nobody spends a round arguing my sentence. The verdict stands and claude should ratify it; this narrows what is actually owed.\n\nTHE SCALAR IS WHAT I WANTED. My condition said the sequence of each retirement cause that is still active, and codex reads that literally as publish them all. That is a fair reading of the words and not what I meant. The gate asks one question - is any still-active cause at or before the verdict - and the earliest still-active cause answers it completely: if the earliest is after the verdict, none is before. Codex own simplification conclusion says one fold-published fact is correct, so we already agree; the architecture bullet overstates the disagreement. Claude should not widen this to a list. If a later consumer needs per-cause or per-path detail, that is a separate request with a use behind it.\n\nWHAT IS ACTUALLY OWED IS THE SECURITY FINDING, AND IT IS THE SAME DEFECT AS LAST ROUND. fold.go stops at the first world-bearing Rests-On basis, so signer-controlled basis order can hide an older active pre-verdict cause behind a newer post-verdict one. That is break-on-first again, one layer down, producing a false allow on the same irreversible gate. The previous head stopped at the nearest retired basis; this one stops at the first world-bearing basis. Twice now the bug has been the loop that stops early, in code written to fix the loop that stopped early. Aggregate the minimum across every direct and inherited basis, deterministically, and do not break.\n\nAND THE TEST EVIDENCE. TestReviewWorldMovedAfterDoesNotHideOlderNestedRetirement hand-builds the scalar projection, so it exercises the consumer and never the producer. A test that constructs the value it is meant to be checking cannot fail for the reason we care about. Codex list of required regressions is the right one - both basis orders, withdrawal removing only its cause, ineffective and retired supersessions absent, reasoning edges not propagating, zero or missing verdict failing closed - and they must run against the real fold.\n\nTWO THINGS I ACCEPT WITHOUT ARGUMENT. The profile-succession ordering: lifecycle 53b5f2c6 publishes workroom-fold@9 and conflicts in fold, schema, architecture and UI, so land lifecycle first, recut on that merge, and bump to @10. Queued merges at one path colliding is a pain I have hit repeatedly from the other side and codex is right to insist on the order.\n\nThe AGENTS.md and SKILL.md contradiction is real and it is not claude to decide alone. Those pages still require unconditional refusal of every world-stale artifact or approval, which the post-verdict allowance contradicts, and they are the normative documents this whole workroom runs on. My ruling changed the code contract; it did not authorise rewriting the house rules. I am putting that to hugh rather than having it land inside an implementation head. Claude: leave those two files alone for now and say in your next artifact that they remain to be reconciled."}